WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.900 --> 00:00:05.780
Whether they were Cobra Kai bullies or Daniel's
inner circle, each member of the Karate Kid

00:00:05.780 --> 00:00:09.060
cast gave something to the production that
helped make it a classic.

00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:12.679
Here's a look at what the stars of the film
have been up to over the years, and what they

00:00:12.680 --> 00:00:13.720
look like today.

00:00:14.080 --> 00:00:18.840
Ralph Macchio got steady work as a young actor
before The Karate Kid, including a prominent

00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:21.260
recurring role on the sitcom Eight Is Enough.

00:00:21.369 --> 00:00:26.999
But 1983 and 1984 brought a powerful one-two
punch that would cement him as an icon of

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:28.200
'80s pop culture.

00:00:28.280 --> 00:00:33.700
In '83 he joined a fraternity of young actors
in The Outsiders, and then in '84 he was cast

00:00:33.700 --> 00:00:36.300
as Daniel LaRusso in Karate Kid.

00:00:36.520 --> 00:00:40.650
The massive success of the latter film led
to three sequels, with Macchio returning for

00:00:40.650 --> 00:00:41.340
two of them.

00:00:41.560 --> 00:00:46.180
Macchio continued to pick up regular acting
work in the '90s and 2000s, including roles

00:00:46.180 --> 00:00:48.780
in My Cousin Vinny and The Secret of NIMH
2.

00:00:49.140 --> 00:00:53.700
He also landed a sweet TV gig in 2008 with
a recurring role on Ugly Betty.

00:00:54.020 --> 00:00:59.640
Besides returning to the Karate Kid franchise
for YouTube's Cobra Kai series in 2018, Macchio's

00:00:59.640 --> 00:01:03.940
also made time to poke fun at himself, playing
versions of "Ralph Macchio" on everything

00:01:03.940 --> 00:01:06.000
from Entourage to How I Met Your Mother.

00:01:06.120 --> 00:01:12.100
"Shut it, Ralph Macchio! …why don't you go have a party with Luke Skywalker, and Harry Potter, and  War Horse,

00:01:12.100 --> 00:01:15.780
And all the other movie bad guys and stop
ruining mine!"

00:01:16.240 --> 00:01:21.040
William Zabka's first major screen acting
work was an episode of The Greatest American

00:01:21.040 --> 00:01:22.580
Hero in 1983.

00:01:22.840 --> 00:01:27.300
His second was the role of Johnny Lawrence
in The Karate Kid, which cemented him as one

00:01:27.300 --> 00:01:31.320
of the best, and perhaps most misunderstood,
villains of 1980s cinema.

00:01:31.750 --> 00:01:36.340
He went on to other major '80s films like
National Lampoon's European Vacation and Back

00:01:36.340 --> 00:01:36.880
to School.

00:01:37.420 --> 00:01:40.420
He also returned briefly as Johnny for The
Karate Kid Part II.

00:01:40.640 --> 00:01:45.740
Zabka continued to work regularly throughout
the '90s and 2000s, particularly in action

00:01:45.740 --> 00:01:49.600
films like Shootfighter: Fight to the Death
and Descent Into Darkness.

00:01:49.980 --> 00:01:55.240
In 2013 he re-entered pop culture mythology
in a major way by playing a version of himself

00:01:55.240 --> 00:01:56.440
in How I Met Your Mother.

00:01:56.660 --> 00:02:01.700
And in 2018 he returned as a down-on-his-luck
Johnny Lawrence for Cobra Kai.

00:02:02.060 --> 00:02:03.200
"It was an illegal kick."

00:02:03.780 --> 00:02:06.460
"Oh Illegal come on, really?  Come on, what about that elbow to my knee?"

00:02:06.520 --> 00:02:07.760
"Yeah, I got a warning you got the win."

00:02:07.940 --> 00:02:10.740
"Woah woah woah.   No fighting
in the showroom, guys."

00:02:11.060 --> 00:02:15.359
Of all the young actors in The Karate Kid,
Elisabeth Shue is the one who arguably came

00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:17.780
away from the film with the highest-profile
career.

00:02:18.069 --> 00:02:21.740
With only a handful of credits to her name
when the film was made, its success granted

00:02:21.740 --> 00:02:24.380
her numerous opportunities in the coming decades.

00:02:24.530 --> 00:02:28.360
Unlike the other leads in the franchise, Shue
did not return for any of the sequels.

00:02:28.370 --> 00:02:33.719
But she did go on to appear in other '80s
classics like Adventures in Babysitting, Cocktail,

00:02:33.720 --> 00:02:37.960
and as the recast role of Jennifer in Back
to the Future Part II and Part III.

00:02:38.720 --> 00:02:43.170
In the '90s she gained acclaim as a major
dramatic talent, earning an Oscar nomination

00:02:43.170 --> 00:02:44.540
for Leaving Las Vegas.

00:02:44.920 --> 00:02:48.700
These days, Shue continues to work regularly,
most prominently in television.

00:02:49.080 --> 00:02:54.159
In 2012 she became one of the stars of CSI,
and she'll next be seen in Amazon's original

00:02:54.160 --> 00:02:55.960
superhero series The Boys.

00:02:56.740 --> 00:03:00.940
Randee Heller had nearly a decade of steady
work in television to her name when she landed

00:03:00.950 --> 00:03:05.059
the role of Daniel's mother Lucille in The
Karate Kid and became one of pop culture's

00:03:05.060 --> 00:03:06.720
most beloved movie moms.

00:03:07.420 --> 00:03:12.190
She returned for The Karate Kid Part II and
Part III, and in between she filled the '80s

00:03:12.190 --> 00:03:17.080
with roles in a variety of TV series with
names like Mama Malone and Boys Will Be Boys.

00:03:17.540 --> 00:03:22.440
Heller has gone on to have an essentially
unbroken streak of roles in major TV series,

00:03:22.440 --> 00:03:24.960
whether one-off guest appearances or recurring
roles.

00:03:25.380 --> 00:03:29.000
Mad Men and Desperate Housewives are just
two of the many shows she's popped up in.

00:03:29.240 --> 00:03:32.600
In 2018, she reprised the role of Lucille
on Cobra Kai.

00:03:33.420 --> 00:03:37.440
Martin Kove had already been acting on screen
for more than a decade by the time The Karate

00:03:37.440 --> 00:03:38.280
Kid came along.

00:03:38.629 --> 00:03:43.800
One of his most important breakout roles came
in 1975, when he was cast as Nero the Hero

00:03:43.800 --> 00:03:46.760
in the Roger Corman-produced Death Race 2000.

00:03:47.189 --> 00:03:52.379
In The Karate Kid, he was flawless as John
Kreese, the ruthless sensei of the Cobra Kai

00:03:52.380 --> 00:03:52.920
dojo.

00:03:53.389 --> 00:03:58.459
Kove returned as Kreese for both Part II and
Part III, and he's continued to act regularly

00:03:58.460 --> 00:03:59.580
in film and TV.

00:04:00.200 --> 00:04:04.859
In 2017, he appeared as "Master John" on a
Karate Kid tribute episode of the '80s-set

00:04:04.859 --> 00:04:10.029
sitcom The Goldbergs, and then a year later,
he reprised the role of John Kreese on Cobra

00:04:10.029 --> 00:04:10.520
Kai.

00:04:11.080 --> 00:04:15.040
One of the things that makes The Karate Kid
such an enduring classic is its willingness

00:04:15.040 --> 00:04:19.360
to add color to supporting characters, particularly
Johnny's friends at Cobra Kai.

00:04:19.540 --> 00:04:23.280
They're never just one-dimensional goons,
and that's particularly true in the case of

00:04:23.280 --> 00:04:24.660
Ron Thomas as Bobby.

00:04:24.980 --> 00:04:28.880
Though he went along with what Johnny and
Kreese asked of him, there was an always some

00:04:28.880 --> 00:04:31.380
reluctance to Bobby's role in bullying Daniel.

00:04:31.660 --> 00:04:34.820
Thomas' acting career is relatively sparse
beyond The Karate Kid.

00:04:35.420 --> 00:04:39.380
But outside of the film world, he's continued
to pour his passion into martial arts.

00:04:39.640 --> 00:04:44.440
Today he's a champion martial artist, martial
arts instructor, author, and motivational

00:04:44.440 --> 00:04:44.940
speaker.

00:04:45.220 --> 00:04:49.660
He holds a sixth-degree black belt, and he's
a National Rank Examiner for the United States

00:04:49.660 --> 00:04:52.380
Judo Association, among many other accolades.

00:04:52.650 --> 00:04:57.340
He's also a ten-year veteran of Universal
Studios Hollywood stunt shows, including the

00:04:57.340 --> 00:05:02.120
"Conan Sword and Sorcery Spectacular" and
the "Miami Vice Action Spectacular."

